      "text": "To solve the equation $7^{x+7} = 8^x$ for $x$, we can rewrite it in a form that makes it easier to compare the bases. Our goal is to get both sides to have the same base, 7 or 8. \n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation:\n\n\\[7^{x+7} = 8^x\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as follows:\n\n\\[7 \\cdot 7^{x} = 8^x\\]\n\nNext, we need to isolate the variable $x$. To do that, we divide both sides by $7^x$:\n\n\\[\\frac{7 \\cdot 7^{x}}{7^x} = \\frac{8^x}{7^x}\\] (By dividing both 7^{x+7} and 8^x by 7^x)\n\nSimplifying the equation, we obtain:\n\n\\[7 = \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)^x\\]\n\nNow, to compare both sides, we express them as powers of the same base. We can do this by letting $\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)^x = 7^{x+m}$ for some $m$. Then by comparing the exponents, we eliminate the left side and obtain:\n\n\\[x + m = \\log_b \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\\]\n\nNow that we have two expressions for $x$ in terms of $m$:\n\n1. $x = \\log_7 (7m)$\n2. $x = \\log_b \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)$\n\nSince both expressions must be equal to $x$, we set them equal to each other:\n\n\\[\\log_7 (7m) = \\log_b \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\\]\n\nWorking with the exponents:\n\n\\[x = \\log_b \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\\]\n\nComparing this with the given solution, we can infer:\n\n\\[x = \\log_b (7^7)\\]\n\nIf these two expressions for $x$ are equivalent, we also solve:\n\n\\[m = x\\]\n\nSimilarly, if the same $m$ we found is squaring, then:\n\n\\[\\frac{7}{8} = b^2\\]\n\nSolving this for $b$:\n\n\\[b = \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{8}}\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{8}}}\\]",
